Done. Added to `Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift`:

- `public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable { case malformedFormat }`
- `public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note`

It splits on `"\n---\n"` into exactly four parts (id, title, pinned flag, body) and throws `malformedFormat` otherwise — no guessing, no defaults, no crash. Tags come from the existing `extractTags(from:)`; `pinned` is `true` only when part 3 equals `"1"`.

**Key detail:** Swift's `split(maxSplits:)` yields `maxSplits + 1` elements, so `maxSplits: 3` mirrors the Rust store's `splitn(4, SEP)`. The body is the final part and may itself contain the separator (e.g. a note body with a literal `\n---\n`), which the Rust `splitn` preserves — the Swift version does too.

**Verified** (via a temporary driver compiled against the source):
- Valid note → correct id/title/pinned/tags/body.
- Body containing the separator → kept intact in `body` (matches Rust `splitn`).
- 2-part text and empty string → throws `malformedFormat`.
- `NoteParseError.malformedFormat == .malformedFormat` → `true`.

`swiftc -typecheck` passes. `swift build`/`swift test` can't run in this environment — SwiftPM's manifest compile fails with `sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted` (a macOS sandbox restriction, unrelated to the code).


